<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?> 

<section xml:id="section_8-3" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Theory-based approach for analyzing paired data</title>

<subsection> 
 <title>Comparing auction formats</title>
<p>An economist at Vanderbilt University devised <url href="http://doi.org/10.1257/aer.89.5.1063">
  a study</url> to compare different types of online auctions. In one experiment he compared a Dutch 
  auction to a first-price sealed bid auction. In the Dutch auction the item for sale starts at a 
  very high price and is lowered gradually until someone finds the price low enough to buy. In 
  the first-price sealed bid auction each bidder submits a single sealed bid before a particular 
  deadline. After the deadline, the person with the highest bid wins. The researcher auctioned 
  off collectible trading cards from the game Magic: The Gathering. He placed pairs of identical 
  cards up for auction; one would go into the Dutch auction and the other to the first-price 
  sealed bid auction. He then looked at the difference in the prices he received on the pair. 
  He repeated this for a total of 88 pairs.</p>

<activity> 
 <!--Question 1-->
  <introduction>
    <p>Before we look at the data that were collected and start the analysis, let's make sure 
    you understand the study design.</p>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Explain why the price data should be analyzed using paired samples as opposed to two independent samples.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Because the researcher paired identical cards and then randomly assigned one card from the pair to the Dutch auction and the other card to the first-price sealed bid auction.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        What makes a pair?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        2 cards
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        What is the explanatory variable? Is it categorical or quantitative?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Type of auction (Dutch or first-price sealed bid); categorical
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        What is the response variable? Is it categorical or quantitative?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Price paid; quantitative
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 2-->
<statement>
<p>State the relevant hypotheses in words. (Use a two-sided alternative.)</p>
</statement>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 3-->
<statement>
<p>Define the parameter of interest and give the symbol that should be assigned to it.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Null hypothesis: The long-run mean price paid for magic cards in a Dutch auction is the same as in a sealed bid auction
  </p>
  <p>
    Alternative hypothesis: The long-run mean price paid for magic cards is different in the two different auction types
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 4-->
<statement>
<p>State the relevant hypotheses in symbols.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Null: <m>\mu_d=0</m>
  </p>
  <p>
    Alternative: <m>\mu_d \not = 0</m>
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity> 
 <!--Question 5-->
  <introduction>
    <p>The data for the auction can be found in the file 
    <url href="https://static-content2.zybooks.com/isi/data/chap7/Auction.txt">
    Auction</url>. The selling prices for the Dutch auction are labeled Dutch 
    and the prices for first-price sealed bid auction are labeled FP. Paste 
    these data into the 
    <url href="https://static-content.zybooks.com/applets/2021/matchedpairs/MatchedPairs.htm?hideExtras=1">
    Matched Pairs applet</url>.</p>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        What is the sample mean price for the Dutch auction?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        5.16
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        What is the sample mean price for the first-price sealed bid auction?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        4.78
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        What are the mean and standard deviation for the difference in the two prices?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        mean = 0.384, standard deviation = 0.675
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine a p-value using the applet. Explain how you did so.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Using 1000 repetitions of the random assignment of the order of the observations, we found a p-value = 0. The p-value was found by counting the proportion of the simulated mean differences that were as extreme as 0.384 (in either direction, beyond).
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

<p>Notice that the simulated null distribution is bell-shaped. This is no coincidence. 
A theory-based method exists that predicts this to occur when certain validity 
conditions are met.</p>

<convention> 
<p>Validity conditions for theory-based analysis of paired data: Theory-based 
methods of inference will work well for paired data if the distribution of 
differences has a symmetric distribution, or you have at least 20 pairs (i.e., 
at least 20 differences) and the distribution of the sample differences is not 
strongly skewed. This test is known as <term>a paired t-test</term>.
</p>
</convention>

<activity>
 <!--Question 6-->
<statement>
<p>Are the validity conditions met for these data? Explain.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Yes, there are 88 pairs without strong skewness in the distribution of differences.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity xml:id="q-8-3-7"> 
 <!--Question 7-->
  <introduction>
    <p>Because the sample is large enough without strong skewness in the distribution of differences, 
    we can use a theory-based approach. We will do this by using the 
    <url href="https://static-content.zybooks.com/applets/2021/tbia/TBIA.html?hideExtras=1">
    Theory-Based Inference applet</url>. To do this:
    <ul>
    <li>Open the <url href="https://static-content.zybooks.com/applets/2021/tbia/TBIA.html?hideExtras=1">Theory-Based Inference applet</url>.</li>
    <li>Choose <em>One mean</em> from the pull-down menu.</li>
    <li>Enter the sample size, sample mean, and sample standard deviation for the differences
    as you found in question 5.</li>
    <li>Check the box for <em>Test of Significance</em>.</li>
    <li>Enter the appropriate information for the hypotheses.</li>
    <li>Make sure the appropriate sign for the alternative hypotheses is chosen.</li>
    <li>Press <em>Calculate</em>.</li>
    </ul>
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        What is the value of the standardized statistic? What does that number tell you?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        t = 5.34, the observed statistic is more than 5 std deviations from the mean of the null.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In the light of the value of the standardized statistic, do you expect the p-value to be small or not small? How are you deciding?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        The p-value should be very small because the observed statistic is very large.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        What is the value of the p-value? Is it similar to the p-value found using the simulation-based method?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        p-value &lt; 0.0001, similar to what we got from simulation
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

<activity xml:id="q-8-3-8"> 
 <!--Question 8-->
  <introduction>
    <p>Recall that the <url href="https://static-content.zybooks.com/applets/2021/tbia/TBIA.html?hideExtras=1">
      Theory-Based Inference applet</url> can also produce confidence intervals for the parameter of interest.
    <ul>
    <li>Go to the applet, check the <em>Confidence interval</em> box and let the confidence level
    be 95%.</li>
    <li>Press <em>Calculate CI</em>.</li>
    </ul>
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Report the 95% confidence interval.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        (0.240, 0.527)
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Interpret the 95% confidence interval in context.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We are 95% confident that on average the selling price of a Magic card in a Dutch auction is more than the selling price of a card in a first-price sealed bid auction, by between $0.240 and $0.527.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Does the 95% confidence interval agree with your conclusion when using the p-value? How are you deciding?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Yes, it does not include 0.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</activity>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="exercises-8-3">
  <title>Exploring Further</title>
  
  

<activity>
 <!--Question 9-->
<statement>
<p>
  Based on the analysis in <xref ref="q-8-3-7"/> and <xref ref="q-8-3-8"/>, state your conclusion in the context of the study. Be sure to comment on:
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        Statistical significance: Do the data provide evidence that selling cards in a Dutch auction differ than when sold in a first-price sealed bid auction on average? How are you deciding? 
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Estimation: Find and interpret a 95% confidence interval.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Causation: Can you conclude causation? If yes, what causes what? If not, how are you deciding?
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Generalization: Can you extend the results of this study? Other kinds of cards? Other types of items? Anything sold in an auction format on the Internet? How are you deciding?
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>     
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    We have very strong evidence of a difference in average prices paid using the two different auction formats because our p-value was &lt; 0.0001.
  </p>
  <p>
    We are 95% confident that the price of the card in the Dutch auction being, on average, between $0.24 and $0.53 higher than the price in the first-price sealed bid auction.
  </p>
  <p> 
    Cause-and-effect is possible here because cards were randomly assigned to treatment groups (type of auction) so we have convincing evidence that the type of auction causes the difference in average prices.
  </p>
  <p> 
    We cannot generalize this result beyond cards similar to those in the study--- there is no guarantee that these 88 pairs of magic cards are somehow representative of “all” magic cards.
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

</subsection>

</section>